  "filingStatusNote": "New Mexico has no state-specific withholding certificate -- FYI-104 itself instructs employees to complete a copy of the FEDERAL Form W-4 and write 'For New Mexico State Withholding Only' across the top. The federal 2020+ W-4's own 3 checkboxes ('Single or Married filing separately' / 'Married filing jointly' / 'Head of household') map 1:1 onto FYI-104's own three lettered tables. This engine reuses certificate.filingStatus with the SAME vocabulary as federalW4 (single/married_joint/married_separate/head_of_household) rather than inventing an NM-specific field; married_separate resolves to the Single table exactly as the federal checkbox bundles it. No certificate on file defaults to Single (the narrowest brackets) -- not itself FYI-104-stated (there is no NM form to fail to file), but consistent with this project's standing least-generous-default convention. FYI-104's own note that 'a married employee who elected withholding at the higher single rate for FEDERAL purposes' must also use the single NM table describes the PRE-2020 W-4's Line 3 checkbox, which no longer exists on the 2020+ form this project's federalW4 model represents -- disclosed as legacy guidance not modelled, not silently ignored.",
  "supplementalWages": {
    "flatRate": 0.059,
    "note": "FYI-104's own instruction: 'If the federal withholding is calculated using a flat percent, a flat 5.9% of the supplemental wage or fringe benefit amount should be withheld for state tax purposes' -- 5.9% is also the top marginal percentage-method rate. Dispatched generically via flatRateSupplementalFromConfig(), the same mechanism NY/other states already use -- zero new code needed. BUG FOUND AND FIXED (a later 'go to every state' pass): newMexicoWithholding() was NOT carving supplemental cash out of its own bracket base, so a bonus was taxed once via NM_SIT's regular bracket AND again via NM_SIT_SUPP's flat 5.9% -- a genuine double-taxation defect, caught by a direct calculatePaycheck() check, not by the existing test suite (the original supplemental test only asserted NM_SIT_SUPP, never NM_SIT, so it couldn't see the bug). Fixed to match computeNYSStyleTax()'s (NY) already-correct pattern: periodWages = fullBase minus supplementalCash."
  },
  "roundFinalToWholeDollar": false,
  "stateQuarterlyHeadFee": {
    "idSuffix": "WC_FEE",
    "name": "Workers' Compensation Fee",
    "employeeQuarterly": 2.0,
    "employerQuarterly": 2.3,
    "exemptEmploymentCategories": [
      "household",
      "agricultural"
    ],
    "$comment": "New Mexico Workers' Compensation Administration FAQ (workerscomp.nm.gov/faqs/, read directly 2026-09-23): 'a quarterly assessment fee of $4.80 for each covered employee working for their business on the last working day of each quarter ($2.25 is paid by the workers and $2.55 is paid by employers)', filed with Taxation and Revenue on the WC-1. Up from $4.30 ($2.00 / $2.30) under TRD's RPD-41108 instructions; the increase applies from 2025-07-01 (TRD notice: \"the WKC fee per employee has increased from $4.30 to $4.80, effective starting July 1, 2025\"), so all of 2026 uses the new amounts. Domestic servants and farm and ranch laborers are exempt (RPD-41108), mapped to the household/agricultural employment categories; real estate salespersons and 10%+ owner-officers aren't representable here, so those employers pass employer.quarterlyHeadFeeCollection.NM = 'skip'. Collected evenly across the quarter's cheques by default, which is what PaycheckCity does ($0.38 semimonthly, $0.75 monthly, matching to the cent)."
  },
  "roundingComment": "FYI-104's own worked example is cent-precision ($21.80) -- no whole-dollar rounding step anywhere in the document.",
  "exemptPretax": [
    "section125",
    "hsa",
    "fsa",
    "dependent_care",
    "deferral_401k",
    "deferral_403b",
    "deferral_457",
    "deferral_simple",
    "commuter"
  ],
  "exemptPretaxComment": "Confirmed via NMSA 7-2-2's own text: New Mexico 'base income' for individual taxpayers is federal adjusted gross income (IRC sec. 62) plus a short specific add-back list untouched by 401(k)/125/HSA/FSA elective deferrals. Same evidentiary tier as Virginia's/West Virginia's/Louisiana's FAGI-conformity citations elsewhere in this project -- one inferential step removed from the withholding wage base itself (FYI-104 doesn't independently define 'wages'), but a genuine direct statute read.",
  "additionalOrReducedWithholding": {
    "rule": "FYI-104's own worked example itself demonstrates additional withholding as a plain add-on ('$21.80 + $20.00 (Additional withholding requested by employee) = $41.80') requested via the same New-Mexico-labeled federal W-4 copy -- already covered generically via certificate.additionalWithholding, the same mechanism every other state in this project uses, zero new code needed.",
    "confidence": "primary_source_confirmed -- FYI-104's own worked example."
  },
  "exemptCertificateNote": "FYI-104 documents specific NM-exempt income categories (enrolled tribal members' income earned and domiciled on their own nation's/pueblo's land; active-duty military pay and certain military spouse income) that should not have NM tax withheld at all, via the federal W-4's own Step 4(c) exempt election -- already covered generically via the existing certificate.exempt mechanism (applyStateWithholdingExemption), zero new code needed.",
  "reciprocity": {
    "exemptWhenDaysWorkedAtMost": 15,
    "$dayCountComment": "FYI-104, quoted directly: 'Employers are not required to withhold New Mexico income tax from wages of nonresident employees working in New Mexico for 15 or fewer days during the calendar year' — so 15 days is itself exempt, unlike Alabama's and Illinois's 'fewer than' phrasing.",
    "$comment": "New Mexico does NOT use this project's usual bilateral-reciprocity mechanism -- FYI-104 states directly: residents are subject to NM tax on ALL income regardless of work location, and 'if that income is also taxed by another state, New Mexico allows a credit for the other state's income tax on that income on their personal income tax return... withholding tax can be reduced or eliminated on such wages' via the employee's own federal-W4-for-NM copy. This is the ordinary return-level-credit baseline this project uses for every other no-reciprocity state, not a special NM mechanism -- reciprocalStates stays empty, no special code needed.",
    "reciprocalStates": []
  },
  "localIncomeTax": {
    "rule": "NONE. New Mexico law does not authorize municipalities or counties to levy a local income/wage tax.",
    "confidence": "cross_source_confirmed via search synthesis; not independently fetched from a specific NM statute barring local income taxation this pass."
  },
  "paidFamilyMedicalLeave": {
    "rule": "NONE mandatory as of 2026. A Paid Family and Medical Leave Act (HB 11, 2025 session -- the 'Welcome Child and Family Wellness Leave Act') cleared committee with a proposed $4/$1,000-wages employer / $5/$1,000-wages employee funding structure, but was not enacted into law this session.",
    "confidence": "cross_source_confirmed via search synthesis, including a look at the bill's own 2025 committee status; not independently fetched from the bill text or NM Legislature's own tracker this pass."
